"A cold cloud core of gas and dust, {d} AU across, turning once every {p} million years. Its own gravity is about to win: press Start.",
"Every parcel falls toward the center. Gas falling near the spin axis reaches the middle and builds the young Sun, a protostar.",
"Gas falling from the sides keeps its spin and cannot reach the center: it swings past and meets gas from the other side of the midplane. A flat disk forms, {r} AU in radius.",
"The collapse is over: {pc} % of the parcels fell onto the young Sun and the rest turn in a disk. The disk's edge goes around {k} times faster than the cloud did.",
"The young Sun warms the disk: hotter near it, colder far out. Inside the frost line ({fl} AU) only rock and metal can be solid; beyond it water freezes too, and there is about four times more solid material.",
"Dust grains stick together into planetesimals, kilometers across. The model starts with {n} of them, spread through the disk like its solids.",
"A core beyond the frost line reached {m} Earth masses while the gas was still there: its gravity pulls in hydrogen and helium, and it becomes a giant planet.",
"Neighbors pull each other in and merge. Growth is fastest near the Sun, where orbits are short and solids are packed close; each protoplanet stops once it has swept up its own ring.",
"The young Sun's light and wind clear away the gas. Left near the Sun: {n} rocky protoplanets, from {m1} to {m2} Earth masses (the Moon is 0.012, Mars 0.107). Their orbits will cross and they will collide: the giant impacts that build Venus and Earth.",
"Loading the giant impact run…",
"{t} million years after the gas cleared, {n} rocky planets are left. The largest has {m} Earth masses at {a} AU (Earth: 1 at 1 AU, Venus: 0.815 at 0.723 AU). This is run {k} of {runs}, the one most like our planets: every run ends differently, because collisions hang on tiny differences in the orbits.",
"This run's {name} took its last giant impact {t} million years after the gas cleared: {mi} Earth masses hit at {v} km/s.",
"The real Mars has only 0.107 Earth masses. Models like this one usually make Mars too big: the small Mars problem, still being worked on.",
"No planet is left near {name}'s orbit in this run: the protoplanets that started near it were thrown out or swept up by their neighbors.",
"Models like this one usually get Mars wrong, too big or missing: the small Mars problem, still being worked on.",
"Impact: bodies of {m1} and {m2} Earth masses collide at {v} km/s ({f} times the speed from just falling together), {ang}° from head on. The model merges them into one body; real impacts also spray debris.",
"The gas is gone. Chapter 3's giant core formed near 3 AU; giant planets can drift through the gas disk as they grow, and this chapter starts Jupiter and Saturn full grown on today's orbits. With them, {n} rocky protoplanets: every body pulls on every other.",
"The gas is gone, and nothing calms the orbits any more. This chapter is an N-body simulation: {n} protoplanets with Jupiter and Saturn, which the model starts full grown on today's orbits. Every body pulls on every other.",
"Tugs from Jupiter and from each other stretch the orbits until they cross. {k} impacts so far, {n} rocky bodies left.",
"tan: Earth's rock · violet: Theia's rock · gray: iron · glowing: shock heated",
"Loading the impact that made the Moon…",
"Theia, a body about the size of Mars ({mi} Earth masses), falls toward the young Earth ({mt} Earth masses). They will meet {ang}° from head on, as fast as falling together from far away makes them: the standard model of the impact that made the Moon (Canup and Asphaug 2001). Each of the {n} particles feels every other's gravity and its neighbors' pressure.",
"Contact. The shock heats the rock (hot material glows). Theia is smeared along Earth's surface, and most of it, iron core and all, is flung on ahead in a long arm.",
"Earth's gravity pulls the arm back: Theia's iron swings around Earth and falls back in.",
"Theia's iron is back inside Earth, sinking toward its core. Flung rock swings around Earth: much falls back, and what stays on orbits that no longer dip into the planet becomes a disk around Earth, mostly rock. Inside the Roche limit (dashed), Earth's tides pull clumps apart; a moon can gather only outside it.",
"{h} hours after contact: a disk of {md} Moon masses circles Earth ({th} % of it from Theia, {fe} % iron), and {me} Moon masses escaped. Within about a year the disk gathers into a moon (drawn, not simulated): with this disk's mass and spin, at most about {mm} Moon masses (the formula of Ida, Canup and Stewart 1997, leaving out the mass lost while the moon gathers; their runs kept a tenth to a half of the disk).",
